00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:04.410
Jack: A coding professional needs
to have strong IT skills and good

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:08.940
knowledge of systems including
hardware, software, and networks.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.359
It is also helpful for them to have a
good understanding of the systems in

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.560
place within the business or area in which
they will be working, and they should be

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.420
fully conversant with coding languages
relevant to their programming area.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.340
Leah: They should also know what coding
languages will work best in relation

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:30.400
to their function such as website
creation, software programming, database

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.560
development or programming operating
systems, security, or data architecture.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.500
The four main job functions of
a programmer are to write, test,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.380
debug and maintain the code that
creates the programmes that computers

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.190
follow to perform their functions.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.660
They are responsible for the design
and development and to ensure that the

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:55.640
software adheres to best practices in
performance, reliability, and security.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:01.110
They can work in developing mobile
apps, coding video games, programming

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.129
websites and can work freelance or in
a multitude of different industries.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.209
Jack: Let's have a look at what other key
skills coding professionals should have.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.699
Like many other complex professions
programming requires a particular

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.100
skill set to be successful.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.500
However, being a coding professional
is not just about knowing all about

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.909
computers and coding language and
programming, there are other key

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.350
skills that are relevant to the role.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.150
Let’s have a look at some of these.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:28.769
Leah: Positive attitude

00:01:29.449 --> 00:01:33.550
When writing code, a positive and
proactive attitude is sometimes needed

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.700
as you may often have to go the extra
mile to create a successful product.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:42.279
A positive attitude is also important
if you need to provide instructions

00:01:42.309 --> 00:01:46.070
or comments on a piece of code, which
should be clear and instructive.

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:51.110
If there are problems, it is important to
be constructive and solution orientated.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.090
Jack: Communication

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:58.729
Coders are often required to write code
so that others including other coders will

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.470
be able to understand and maintain it.

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:04.860
They will also need to be able to
explain complex concepts, like how

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.359
something works, or the reasons
behind things to non-coders like

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.680
clients in relatively simple terms.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.140
The ability to communicate effectively,
can make all the difference in

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.010
getting their ideas over to others.

00:02:18.429 --> 00:02:19.119
Leah: Teamwork

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.560
Most developers are part of a team
and should be able to share their

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.860
ideas and support the ideas of others.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:29.270
This may involve disagreements and
debate, but this can help develop

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.380
differing ideas to reach a common goal.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.890
Members of the team may have different
functions, but it may involve them all

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:41.000
working at different stages of the process
to ultimately ensure a successful outcome.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:42.980
Jack: Time Management

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:47.920
Time management is essential for a coding
professional not getting distracted from

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.110
the task at hand, knowing what is needed,
and being able to stick to deadlines and

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.290
produce high quality work consistently.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.279
The ability to meet targets and get it
right the first time is the best approach,

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:03.050
extra features can be added later
when the programme is up and running.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.190
Leah: Problem solving

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.230
A coder should be good at problem
solving because this is what they do,

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.410
understanding how to solve problems
within the software they are programming.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.100
It doesn't matter how knowledgeable
they are, they may come across something

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.310
that is far from straightforward
and needs to be worked out.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.040
Being able to break down a problem
into its component parts, then

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.440
finding a solution, is a vital skill.

00:03:27.559 --> 00:03:28.609
Jack: Self-motivation

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.890
Learning to code can be difficult,
so a programmer must be able to

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:36.320
motivate themselves to stick to
it even when things go wrong.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.730
They also need to have patience, so as not
to get stressed when de bugging code or

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.720
working with a difficult system or client.

00:03:45.309 --> 00:03:46.540
Leah: Analytical skills

00:03:47.309 --> 00:03:50.320
Code must be visualised to work
out what outcomes need to be

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.370
created and the processes involved.

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:56.630
Coders need to draw analytical
conclusions from things that may on

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.330
the surface seem straightforward.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:02.970
Curiosity can also help a coder push
themselves to try new approaches.

